1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,221
This is ECE 358, video 25 b. We solve the second half of the handout: Selected Problems from Previous Midterm Exams.

2
00:00:11,671 --> 00:00:20,930
Question 2 has five short parts: TCP and UDP, applications, port numbers, HTTP, and Go-Back-N.

3
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:25,689
Question 5 is about errors and the Selective Repeat window.

4
00:00:26,139 --> 00:00:30,171
Question 6 is about TCP sequence and ACK numbers.

5
00:00:30,621 --> 00:00:38,280
The handout gives official answers for questions 2 and 6. For question 5, it refers to the lecture notes.

6
00:00:38,730 --> 00:00:47,157
Question 2, part a. What is the difference between the services of TCP and UDP? Pause the video and try it.

7
00:00:47,607 --> 00:00:59,767
TCP first. Before data transfer, TCP sets up a connection. The client and the server exchange three control messages: SYN, SYN ACK and ACK.

8
00:01:00,217 --> 00:01:10,350
TCP gives reliable, in-order delivery. The receiver sends ACKs. When a segment is lost, the sender retransmits it after a timeout.

9
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:15,280
TCP also gives flow control and congestion control.

10
00:01:15,730 --> 00:01:19,677
UDP is connectionless. There is no setup and no ACK.

11
00:01:20,127 --> 00:01:28,063
It is a best-effort service. Segments can be lost or arrive out of order, and UDP does not retransmit them.

12
00:01:28,513 --> 00:01:40,566
The official answer says the same: TCP is connection-oriented and reliable, with flow control and congestion control. UDP is connectionless and best effort.

13
00:01:41,016 --> 00:02:01,112
The official answer also says that UDP has no error detection. But the slides show a checksum in the UDP header. It detects bit errors. UDP does not recover from them. So write: UDP detects bit errors with its checksum, but it does not correct or retransmit.

14
00:02:01,562 --> 00:02:09,285
Part b. Choose TCP or UDP for four applications, and explain. Pause the video and try it.

15
00:02:09,735 --> 00:02:21,084
Slide 12 of chapter 2 gives the rule. Some applications need 100 percent reliable data. They use TCP, which retransmits lost data.

16
00:02:21,534 --> 00:02:30,537
Other applications need low delay and can tolerate some loss. They use UDP, because a retransmission arrives too late.

17
00:02:30,987 --> 00:02:37,621
One. File transfer needs every byte. A lost byte changes the file. So use TCP.

18
00:02:38,071 --> 00:02:55,479
Two. A real-time streamed video needs low delay. There is no time to retransmit errors, and a small loss is acceptable. So use UDP. Slide 22 of chapter 3 lists streaming multimedia as a use of UDP.

19
00:02:55,929 --> 00:03:06,873
Three. Web browsing needs pages without errors, so that all content shows correctly. So use TCP. HTTP runs over TCP.

20
00:03:07,323 --> 00:03:19,419
Four. A VoIP call has strict timing needs. A retransmission adds too much delay. So use UDP. These four answers match the official answers.

21
00:03:19,869 --> 00:03:23,880
Part c. What are port numbers for? Pause the video and try it.

22
00:03:24,330 --> 00:03:38,645
One host runs many application processes, for example a web server, an FTP server and RIP. Each process has a socket, and each socket has a port number. Port numbers have 16 bits.

23
00:03:39,095 --> 00:03:42,913
All segments for this host arrive through one network layer.

24
00:03:43,363 --> 00:03:48,654
The transport layer reads the destination port number in the segment header: 80.

25
00:03:49,104 --> 00:04:00,603
It gives the data to the socket with port 80, the web server. This is demultiplexing. At the sender, the transport layer adds the port numbers. This is multiplexing.

26
00:04:01,053 --> 00:04:08,029
So the official answer is: many higher-layer protocols share one TCP or UDP layer.

27
00:04:08,479 --> 00:04:14,601
The port number identifies the higher-layer protocol, or process, that the data is for.

28
00:04:15,051 --> 00:04:22,774
Part d. What is the difference between persistent and non-persistent HTTP? Pause the video and try it.

29
00:04:23,224 --> 00:04:40,248
Non-persistent HTTP first. The client opens a TCP connection: one RTT. Then it sends GET and receives object 1: one more RTT. That is 2 RTTs. Then the connection closes.

30
00:04:40,698 --> 00:04:49,679
For object 2, the client opens a new connection. Again 2 RTTs. So each object needs 2 RTTs.

31
00:04:50,129 --> 00:04:55,249
Persistent HTTP leaves the connection open after the response.

32
00:04:55,699 --> 00:05:11,486
The client sends the next request over the same connection. Object 2 needs only 1 RTT. So persistent HTTP saves one RTT, and the OS overhead, for each object after the first.

33
00:05:11,936 --> 00:05:25,824
So the official answer is: persistent HTTP uses one TCP connection for many objects. Non-persistent HTTP needs one TCP connection per object.

34
00:05:26,274 --> 00:05:46,114
Part e. What is the difference between Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat? Pause the video and try it. Both senders can have up to N unACKed packets in the pipeline. Use the example of slide 34. The sender sends packets 0 to 7, and packets 4 and 6 are lost.

35
00:05:46,564 --> 00:05:53,007
In Go-Back-N, the receiver does not buffer out-of-order packets. It discards 5 and 7.

36
00:05:53,457 --> 00:06:01,755
For each packet, it ACKs the last in-order packet: 0, 1, 2, 3, then 3 and 3. These are cumulative ACKs.

37
00:06:02,205 --> 00:06:13,747
The Go-Back-N sender has one timer, for the oldest unACKed packet. When it expires, the sender retransmits all unACKed packets: 4, 5, 6 and 7.

38
00:06:14,197 --> 00:06:26,229
In Selective Repeat, the receiver ACKs each correctly received packet: 0, 1, 2, 3, 5 and 7. It buffers 5 and 7 for in-order delivery later.

39
00:06:26,679 --> 00:06:39,863
The Selective Repeat sender has one timer for each unACKed packet. When a timer expires, it retransmits only that packet: 4, and later 6. This matches the official answer.

40
00:06:40,313 --> 00:06:59,342
Question 5. Nodes A and B use Selective Repeat over one link. K is the size of the sequence number space. W s and W r are the sender and receiver window sizes. The handout says: see the lecture notes for the answer. Pause the video and try it.

41
00:06:59,792 --> 00:07:16,027
Part a. Slide 26 of chapter 3 names four errors of an unreliable channel. Remember them as BOLD. B is bit errors: the channel can corrupt a packet. A checksum detects it, and the receiver discards the packet.

42
00:07:16,477 --> 00:07:24,263
O is out of order: packets can arrive in a different order. Sequence numbers let the receiver put them back in order.

43
00:07:24,713 --> 00:07:32,009
L is loss: a packet can disappear. A timer at the sender detects the loss, and the sender retransmits.

44
00:07:32,459 --> 00:07:44,662
D is duplication: the same packet can arrive twice. Sequence numbers let the receiver find the copy. ACKs give the feedback from the receiver to the sender for all of this.

45
00:07:45,112 --> 00:07:59,085
Part b. Use the example of slide 48. The sequence numbers are 0, 1, 2 and 3, so K is 4. Both windows have size 3. The sender sends packets 0, 1 and 2.

46
00:07:59,535 --> 00:08:03,845
The receiver gets all three, and it delivers them to the upper layer.

47
00:08:04,295 --> 00:08:13,852
After each packet, the receiver window moves by one. After packet 2, the receiver accepts the sequence numbers 3, 0 and 1.

48
00:08:14,302 --> 00:08:18,526
But all three ACKs are lost. So the sender window does not move.

49
00:08:18,976 --> 00:08:24,821
The timer of packet 0 expires. The sender retransmits the old packet 0.

50
00:08:25,271 --> 00:08:40,183
Sequence number 0 is in the receiver window 3, 0, 1. So the receiver accepts the old copy as new data. It delivers the data of packet 0 twice. This is duplicate data at the upper layer.

51
00:08:40,633 --> 00:08:51,172
Part c. Look at the worst case. The sender may still resend its oldest W s sequence numbers. With windows of 3, these are 0, 1 and 2.

52
00:08:51,622 --> 00:08:58,854
If all ACKs are lost, the receiver already accepts the next W r numbers: 3, 0 and 1.

53
00:08:59,304 --> 00:09:13,960
3 plus 3 is 6 numbers, in a space of only 4. So the numbers 0 and 1 are in both groups. A packet with sequence number 0 can be an old copy or new data. The receiver cannot tell.

54
00:09:14,410 --> 00:09:29,493
With windows of 2, the two groups use 2 plus 2, that is 4 numbers: 0, 1 and 2, 3. Each number has one meaning. The old packet 0 is not in the receiver window, so the receiver does not accept it.

55
00:09:29,943 --> 00:09:35,788
So the two groups must not overlap: W s plus W r must be at most K.

56
00:09:36,238 --> 00:09:52,729
Selective Repeat uses equal windows. Then 2 W s must be at most K, so W s must be at most K over 2. This is the rule of slide 48: the window size is at most half of the sequence number space.

57
00:09:53,179 --> 00:09:58,789
In the example, K is 4, so the window can be at most 4 over 2, that is 2.

58
00:09:59,239 --> 00:10:08,114
Question 6. Hosts A and B use a TCP connection. B has received all bytes from A up to byte 126.

59
00:10:08,564 --> 00:10:21,791
A sends two segments back to back. Segment 1 has 80 bytes, and its sequence number is 127. Its source port is 302, and its destination port is 80.

60
00:10:22,241 --> 00:10:30,753
So segment 1 carries bytes 127 to 127 plus 80 minus 1, that is 206.

61
00:10:31,203 --> 00:10:40,995
Segment 2 has 40 bytes. B sends an ACK for every segment. Find the numbers in parts a, b and c. Pause the video and try it.

62
00:10:41,445 --> 00:10:57,658
Part a. A TCP sequence number is the byte-stream number of the first byte in the segment. Segment 1 starts at byte 127 and carries 80 bytes. So it carries bytes 127 to 206.

63
00:10:58,108 --> 00:11:03,975
The header of segment 1 has source port 302 and destination port 80.

64
00:11:04,425 --> 00:11:15,497
Segment 2 starts right after segment 1, at byte 127 plus 80, which is 207. So its sequence number is 207.

65
00:11:15,947 --> 00:11:26,357
It uses the same connection, so the ports do not change: source port 302, and destination port 80. This matches the official answer.

66
00:11:26,807 --> 00:11:37,453
Part b. Segment 1 arrives first. It carries bytes 127 to 206. Now B has all bytes up to 206.

67
00:11:37,903 --> 00:11:49,209
A TCP ACK number is the sequence number of the next byte that the receiver expects. That is 206 plus 1, so the ACK number is 207.

68
00:11:49,659 --> 00:12:00,497
The ACK goes from B back to A, so the ports swap. The source port is 80, and the destination port is 302. This matches the official answer.

69
00:12:00,947 --> 00:12:15,816
Part c. Now segment 2 arrives first. It starts at byte 207 and has 40 bytes. So it carries bytes 207 to 207 plus 40 minus 1, that is 246.

70
00:12:16,266 --> 00:12:25,695
Bytes 127 to 206 are still missing. This is a gap. In practice, TCP buffers the out-of-order bytes.

71
00:12:26,145 --> 00:12:44,151
When an out-of-order segment arrives, the receiver at once sends a duplicate ACK. It holds the sequence number of the next expected byte. So the ACK number is 127: B still waits for byte 127. This matches the official answer.

72
00:12:44,601 --> 00:12:57,571
The question does not ask for more. But when segment 1 arrives later, it fills the gap. Then B sends a cumulative ACK, 246 plus 1, that is 247.

73
00:12:58,021 --> 00:13:15,088
Here is the recap. One. TCP sets up a connection and gives reliable, in-order delivery, flow control and congestion control. UDP is connectionless and best effort. Its checksum detects bit errors, but it does not recover.

74
00:13:15,538 --> 00:13:25,650
Two. An application that needs every byte uses TCP. An application that needs low delay and tolerates loss uses UDP.

75
00:13:26,100 --> 00:13:30,367
Three. Port numbers identify the process that the data is for.

76
00:13:30,817 --> 00:13:42,209
Four. Non-persistent HTTP uses one TCP connection per object. Persistent HTTP uses one connection for many objects.

77
00:13:42,659 --> 00:13:56,909
Five. Go-Back-N uses cumulative ACKs and one timer, and resends all unACKed packets. Selective Repeat uses individual ACKs and a timer per packet, and resends only the lost packet.

78
00:13:57,359 --> 00:14:08,111
Six. The four errors are bit errors, out of order, loss and duplicates. In Selective Repeat, the window is at most half of the sequence number space.

79
00:14:08,561 --> 00:14:20,807
Seven. A TCP sequence number is the number of the first byte. The ACK number is the next byte expected, and the ACK swaps the ports. That is the end of video 25.
